//? Title: Conjugate Gradient: Directions, Residuals and Reliable Stopping
//? Subtitle: Actual Krylov histories, a Dirichlet Laplacian and the limits of finite termination
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a symmetric positive-definite matrix or linear operator A and a right-hand
side b, find x with Ax=b without factoring the matrix. Large sparse systems make
repeated matrix-vector products attractive, but a small recursively updated
residual can conceal error in floating arithmetic. Conjugate gradient builds
conjugate search directions and minimises the quadratic over growing Krylov
spaces. This report works through two-dimensional steps, separates energy error
from residual norm, reproduces the original twenty-unknown Laplacian comparison
and checks the role of the spectrum. Native datamunge execution histories retain
the actual operands and count both recurrence and residual-check products.
)

\toc

> The method: minimise a positive quadratic

For a real symmetric positive-definite A, the unique minimiser of

\[
\phi(x)=\tfrac12 x^\top Ax-b^\top x
\]
\alttext(The quadratic is half x transpose A x minus b transpose x.)

solves Ax=b. Its gradient is Ax−b=−r, correcting the sign in the original report.
For an iterate x and direction p, differentiating φ(x+αp) gives the exact line
minimiser α=pᵀr/(pᵀAp). CG starts with x₀=0, r₀=p₀=b and uses

\[
\alpha_k=\frac{r_k^\top r_k}{p_k^\top Ap_k},\qquad
x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kp_k,\qquad
r_{k+1}=r_k-\alpha_kAp_k,
\]
\alttext(The step coefficient is the squared residual norm divided by directional curvature; the iterate and residual receive their corresponding updates.)
\[
\beta_k=\frac{r_{k+1}^\top r_{k+1}}{r_k^\top r_k},\qquad
p_{k+1}=r_{k+1}+\beta_kp_k.
\]
\alttext(The ratio of successive squared residual norms supplies the previous-direction contribution to the new direction.)

In exact arithmetic, the residuals are mutually orthogonal and the nonzero
directions are A-conjugate: pᵢᵀApⱼ=0 for i≠j. Inductively, rₖ is orthogonal
to the preceding Krylov space Kₖ(A,b)=span(b,Ab,…,Aᵏ⁻¹b), and xₖ minimises
φ over that space. SPD curvature makes the minimiser unique. There are at most
n independent directions, proving termination within n steps in exact arithmetic.
This proof does not give a floating-point iteration guarantee.

> Two directions that can be checked by hand

Take A=diag(1,4) and b=(1,1). The answer is x*=(1,1/4).
The first α is 2/5, so x₁=(0.4,0.4), r₁=(0.6,−0.6), β₀=0.36 and
p₁=(0.96,−0.24). Its curvature product with p₀ is 0.96−0.96=0;
the second line minimisation reaches the answer in exact arithmetic. The diagram
draws level sets of the squared energy error (x−x*)ᵀA(x−x*) and the actual
recorded path, rather than reconstructing a separate CG run for plotting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cg_directions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> a{{1,0},{0,4}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::conjugate_gradient_trace(a,{1,1});
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(240,1000);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke contour;contour.color={148,163,184};contour.width=1.1;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke path;path.color={37,99,235};path.width=2.4;
  for(double level:{.05,.2,.8,2.})
    d.ellipse({1,.25},std::sqrt(level),std::sqrt(level)/2,{255,255,255,0},contour);
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> points{{0,0}};
  for(const auto& step:t.steps) points.push_back({step.x[0],step.x[1]});
  d.path(points,path);
  for(const auto& point:points) d.ellipse(point,.025,.025,{37,99,235},path);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke leader;leader.color={100,116,139};leader.width=.8;
  d.path({{0,0},{-.2,-.61}},leader)
   .path({{.4,.4},{.05,1.02}},leader)
   .path({{1,.25},{1.9,-.61}},leader)
   .text({-.2,-.73},"x0 = (0, 0)",18)
   .text({.05,1.14},"x1 = (0.4, 0.4)",18)
   .text({1.9,-.73},"x* = (1, 0.25)",18)
   .text({1,1.6},"Actual CG steps on an SPD quadratic",24)
   .text({1,-1.1},"Contours: squared energy error; directions are A-conjugate",20)
   .save_svg("build/cg_directions.svg");
  std::print("| step | alpha | beta used | x[0] | x[1] | recursive norm | recomputed norm |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps)
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n",s.iteration,s.alpha,s.has_next_direction?std::format("{:.6f}",s.beta):"none",s.x[0],s.x[1],s.recursive_residual,s.true_residual);
  long double conjugacy=0;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<2;++i) conjugacy+=static_cast<long double>(t.steps[0].p[i])*t.steps[1].ap[i];
  std::print("\nActual p0^T A p1 = {:.3e}; {} completed steps, {} operator products.\n",conjugacy,t.solution.iterations,t.products);
}
```
