| 1 | 0.400000 | 0.360000 | 0.400000 | 0.400000 | 8.485e-01 | 8.485e-01 |
| 2 | 0.625000 | none | 1.000000 | 0.250000 | 0.000e+00 | 0.000e+00 |

Actual p0^T A p1 = 0.000e+00; 2 completed steps, 4 operator products.
\image(figures/cg_directions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two actual line-minimising steps on A=diag(1,4). Equal coordinate scales preserve the geometry; grey ellipses are energy-error level sets.)
\alttext(A blue two-segment path goes from the origin through point 0.4,0.4 to the ellipse centre at 1,0.25. The contour ellipses are twice as wide as they are tall.)

The recorded last β is absent because no next direction is constructed. The
history contains owned vectors x, r, p and Ap from the executing kernel; changing
the caller's matrix or a different snapshot cannot alter them.

> Energy error decreases; the residual norm can rise

Writing e=x−x*, subtracting the minimum quadratic value yields
φ(x)−φ(x*)=½eᵀAe. Exact CG therefore decreases the energy norm ||e||A.
It does not minimise the Euclidean residual norm ||b−Ax||₂ at every step.
For A=diag(1,100), b=(1,0.1), the first α=1.01/2=0.505 makes
r₁=(0.495,−4.95): the residual grows while the energy error falls.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cg_energy_residual.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::conjugate_gradient_trace(
    std::vector<std::vector<double>>{{1,0},{0,100}},{1,.1});
  const double initial_energy=std::hypot(1.,.01);
  std::vector<double> iteration{0},residual{1},energy{1};
  std::print("| step | recomputed residual / initial | energy error / initial |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 1 | 1 |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) {
    iteration.push_back(s.iteration);
    residual.push_back(double(s.true_residual/t.initial_residual));
    energy.push_back(std::hypot(s.x[0]-1,10*(s.x[1]-.001))/initial_energy);
    std::print("| {} | {:.6e} | {:.6e} |\n",s.iteration,residual.back(),energy.back());
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(iteration,residual,"relative to initial residual",{220,38,38},2)
      .points(iteration,residual,"",{220,38,38},5)
      .line(iteration,energy,"relative to initial energy error",{37,99,235},2)
      .points(iteration,energy,"",{37,99,235},5)
      .legend({"residual norm / initial","energy error / initial"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Residual growth can accompany energy improvement")
      .x_label("completed CG step").y_label("norm divided by its own initial value")
      .y_limits(0,6.2).size(900,560).save_svg("build/cg_energy_residual.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| step | recomputed residual / initial | energy error / initial |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 4.950000e+00 | 7.000007e-01 |
| 2 | 3.866496e-16 | 3.902933e-17 |
\image(figures/cg_energy_residual.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first residual norm increases by nearly a factor of five, while the energy error decreases. Each curve is normalised by its own initial norm; the quantities have different meanings.)
\alttext(The red normalised residual rises from one to almost five before falling close to zero. The blue normalised energy error falls from one at each step.)

For SPD A, λmin||e||₂≤||r||₂≤λmax||e||₂ in exact arithmetic.
A small residual alone therefore gives no scale-free small forward-error claim.
The familiar exact-arithmetic energy bound, with κ=λmax/λmin, is

\[
\frac{\|e_k\|_A}{\|e_0\|_A}
\le 2\left(\frac{\sqrt\kappa-1}{\sqrt\kappa+1}\right)^k.
\]
\alttext(The relative energy error is bounded by twice a condition-number-dependent factor to the kth power.)

To see why, any Krylov iterate has error qₖ(A)e₀ for a polynomial of degree
at most k with qₖ(0)=1. CG chooses the smallest energy error in that space.
Bounding ||qₖ(A)e₀||A by max over the spectrum of |qₖ| times ||e₀||A and
choosing a scaled Chebyshev polynomial on [λmin,λmax] gives this bound.
It is a worst-case envelope; eigenvalue locations and active RHS components
can make observed convergence much faster. Rounding can invalidate exact
orthogonality, conjugacy and the finite-termination argument.

> Original example: twenty interior Dirichlet unknowns

The unscaled matrix L=tridiag(−1,2,−1) with homogeneous boundary values is SPD:
xᵀLx=Σ from i=0 to n of (xᵢ₊₁−xᵢ)² with x₀=xₙ₊₁=0; only the zero
vector makes every term vanish. For unit RHS its exact discrete solution is
xᵢ=i(n+1−i)/2, using one-based interior i. Substitution into the second
difference proves the formula and supplies an independent answer.

Both displayed histories below come from native execution. CG and the legacy
conjugate_gradient call share a kernel; Gauss-Seidel and gauss_seidel_trace share
a forward in-place sweep. The latter performs forty sweeps unless it attains an
exact computed zero. Both use a zero starting point. No separate report solver
reconstructs the plotted results.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/conjugate_gradient.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::laplacian_1d_matrix(20);
  const std::vector<double> b(20,1);
  const auto cg=dm::algorithms::conjugate_gradient_trace(a,b);
  const auto gs=dm::algorithms::gauss_seidel_trace(a,b,40,0);
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::laplacian_1d_unit_solution(20);
  const auto legacy=dm::algorithms::conjugate_gradient(a,b);
  std::print("| method | completed updates | final recomputed norm | converged |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  std::print("| CG | {} | {:.6e} | {} |\n| forward Gauss-Seidel | {} | {:.6e} | {} |\n",cg.solution.iterations,cg.solution.residual,cg.solution.converged,gs.solution.iterations,gs.solution.residual,gs.solution.converged);
  std::print("\nLegacy call equals trace solution: {}. CG operator products: {}.\n",legacy.x==cg.solution.x,cg.products);
  std::print("\n| interior index | CG x | analytical x | GS after 40 sweeps |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i:{0,4,9,10,15,19})
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",i+1,cg.solution.x[i],exact[i],gs.solution.x[i]);
  std::vector<double> ci{0},cr{std::log10(double(cg.initial_residual))},gi,gr;
  for(const auto& s:cg.steps) {
    ci.push_back(s.iteration);cr.push_back(std::log10(std::max(double(s.true_residual),1e-16)));
  }
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<gs.residuals.size();++k) {
    gi.push_back(k);gr.push_back(std::log10(std::max(gs.residuals[k],1e-16)));
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(gi,gr,"Gauss-Seidel",{220,38,38},2)
      .line(ci,cr,"CG",{37,99,235},2).points(ci,cr,"",{37,99,235},5)
      .legend({"forward Gauss-Seidel","CG: checked residual"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Original 20-unknown Laplacian, unit right-hand side")
      .x_label("completed step or forward sweep").y_label("log10 recomputed residual; display floor 1e-16")
      .y_limits(-17,3).size(900,580).save_svg("build/conjugate_gradient.svg");
}
