| 160 | 80 | 160 | 0.000e+00 | 0.000e+00 |
\image(figures/cg_laplacian_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The unit-RHS symmetry control takes n/2 steps and n total operator requests in these runs. Each product is O(n), so this particular unpreconditioned sequence has quadratic total work; the plot does not establish constant iteration counts.)
\alttext(A single blue line records total matrix-free requests for twenty, forty, eighty and one hundred sixty unknowns. The requests increase proportionally with dimension.)

The general implementation performs one Ap for the recurrence and one Ax to
check the resulting iterate at every completed step. Those diagnostic products
are included in products. An unsuccessful curvature attempt adds one Ap without
a completed step. Thus dense checked solving costs O(n²+kn²) after input
validation, and a sparse operator with product cost T(n) costs O(k(T(n)+n)).
The solve-only path retains O(n) vector scratch; a trace stores four n-vectors
per step, O(kn) additional entries. The displayed work is not a wall-clock
ranking or a complete operation-by-operation equality between CG and GS.

For this Laplacian κ=cot²(π/(2(n+1))) grows quadratically in n. Preconditioning
is often needed for larger problems; this API demonstrates unpreconditioned CG.
No external dataset is needed: the analytic solution and controllable spectrum
give stronger checks of the numerical claims.

> Input contracts, stopping and honest failure

The tolerance is absolute and inclusive. A zero RHS or empty system can converge
initially; a zero iteration budget returns x=0 with residual ||b||, without
claiming convergence unless that norm is within tolerance. All finite entries,
dimensions, controls and exact stored symmetry are validated before dense work.
SPD remains a precondition: detecting positive curvature on visited directions
does not certify the entire matrix. A matrix-free callback must itself be a
deterministic linear SPD map and return a finite matching vector.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::algorithms;
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> a{{4,1},{1,3}};
  const auto zero=conjugate_gradient_trace(a,{0,0});
  const auto capped=conjugate_gradient_trace(a,{1,2},{1,0,0});
  const auto negative=conjugate_gradient_trace(std::vector<std::vector<double>>{{-1}},{1});
  std::print("| control | completed steps | operator requests | converged | recomputed residual |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,r]:std::array<std::pair<std::string_view,ConjugateGradientRun>,3>{{{"zero RHS",zero},{"one-step budget",capped},{"negative curvature",negative}}})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",name,r.solution.iterations,r.products,r.solution.converged,r.solution.residual);
  std::print("\nNegative curvature has explicit reason: {}.\n",negative.stop==CGStop::nonpositive_curvature);
  try { (void)conjugate_gradient_trace(a,{1,2},{100,1e-10,15});return 1; }
  catch(const std::length_error&) { std::print("A 15-entry trace cap rejects the 16-entry two-step history.\n"); }
  try { (void)conjugate_gradient_trace(std::vector<std::vector<double>>{{2,1},{0,2}},{0,0});return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { std::print("Nonsymmetry is rejected even for zero RHS.\n"); }
  const auto tiny=conjugate_gradient_solve(std::vector<std::vector<double>>{{2}},{1e-200},{4,0,0});
  std::print("Tiny RHS solves without squared-double underflow: {}; x={:.1e}.\n",tiny.solution.converged,tiny.solution.x[0]);
}
```
