// result_end

The recursive vector r is separately retained from the recomputed b−Ax norm.
If the former reaches tolerance or collapses to zero while the latter does not,
CGStop::residual_gap stops unconverged. This implementation does not silently
restart, replace residuals or use a relative stopping rule. Nonfinite or
unrepresentable arithmetic throws. Inner products and norms widen to long
double; vector/operator output remains double. The recomputed norm is based on
the rounded returned Ax and widened differences: it is a floating diagnostic,
not an interval certificate of the mathematical residual.

A positive max_trace_values caps actual stored scalar entries, checked after
positive curvature and before updating the next iterate. A cap failure returns
no partial result; callback effects already performed are not rolled back. Zero
means unlimited. Solve-only calls ignore this optional storage cap. Histories
are actual execution records and never obtain their states by rerunning prefixes.

> Conclusion

Conjugate directions explain the small worked solution and the ten-step unit-RHS
Laplacian result. The energy interpretation and active-spectrum polynomial explain
more than an assertion that every residual must decrease. Native histories now
show the actual recurrence and recomputed residual, with complete operator costs,
validated contracts and explicit unconverged stops. Finite termination belongs
to exact arithmetic; the residual check and analytic controls establish what
these particular floating runs achieved.

> References

- M. R. Hestenes and E. Stiefel (1952), *Methods of Conjugate Gradients for Solving Linear Systems*, Journal of Research of the National Bureau of Standards 49, 409–436. [Original NIST paper](https://math.nist.gov/mcsd/Reports/2002/hestenes-steifel-52.pdf), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.049.044).
- R. Barrett et al., *Templates for the Solution of Linear Systems: Building Blocks for Iterative Methods*, second edition. [Author-maintained Netlib text](https://netlib.org/templates/templates.html), sections on CG, Gauss–Seidel and stopping in floating arithmetic. The explicit true-residual checks and owned histories here are documented implementation additions.
